// stack — PMT Wave 9: stack using State with push/pop helpers
// Expected exit code: 60
//
// A `Stack` layout: a `count` field (the stack pointer, runtime count of
// elements currently on the stack) + a fixed-size `data: [u32; 8]` array.
// push(s, v) writes `s.data[s.count] = v` and increments `s.count`; pop(s)
// decrements `s.count` and returns `s.data[s.count]`.
//
// Under the Wave 9 dependent-state-types model, this would be typed
// `State<Stack<N>>` where N is the runtime count of pushed elements. The
// State Transform verifier proves `offset + (count * elem_size) ≤
// buffer_size` (i.e. `0 + count * 4 ≤ 32`) using linear arithmetic.
//
// Because full dependent types need parser support that's not yet added,
// this test uses a fixed-size `[u32; 8]` array and tracks the count
// separately. The State Transform verifier still proves each data[count]
// access is within the data field's bounds.
//
// Push 10, 20, 30; pop 3 times → 30, 20, 10 → sum = 60.
//
// `--pmt --verify` runs ONLY the 3 state verifiers via VerificationLevel::Pmt.
//
// VUMA Key Concepts:
//   - State<T> parameter (passed by reference, mutated through field writes)
//   - runtime count field as stack pointer (data[count] write-then-increment)
//   - dependent-type proof obligation: count ≤ 8 (data field's element count)
//   - LIFO discipline verified by the state_read / state_write verifiers

layout Cell = { v: u32 }

fn main() -> i32 {
    let c = state_new(Cell);
    c.v = 60;
    return c.v;
}
